; ===========================================================================
; Where the light comes from.
;
; One sun and one flat ambient, which is all flat-shaded palette art wants. What
; they are WORTH is here, because it turned out to be a decision about how the
; game looks rather than a fact about drawing it. The first player to see this
; said the map looked flat, twice, and he was right: the sun stood nearly
; overhead, so every surface facing up caught the same light and the floor plan
; read as a picture of a floor plan.
;
; ★ PITCH IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS, and what it separates is the UPRIGHT from the
; FLAT -- a wall from the ground it stands on. It cannot separate a floor from
; the lid on top of a wall, because those are the same tile facing the same way
; and no angle of light will ever shade them differently. That is what CapTint
; is for, down in the themes.
;
; At 57 -- where this used to stand -- a floor came out at 1.09 of full
; brightness and clipped to white, while the wall beside it sat at 0.79. Flat and
; blown out at once. At 40 the floor is 0.89 and a wall facing the sun is 1.02,
; so the wall is the bright thing and the ground is what it stands on.
;
; AmbientPercent is the sun's opposite and wants reading beside it: it is the
; whole of what keeps an unlit face from being a black shape, and the whole of
; what flattens the picture again if it goes too high. At 100 every face is lit
; the same whichever way it points, which is exactly what Pitch is here to stop.
;
; Look and nothing else -- the simulation never reads a word of this.
; ===========================================================================
Sun
  ; Degrees above the horizon. 90 is noon and draws no relief whatever.
  Pitch = 40
  ; Degrees round the compass, which decides which side of a wall is the lit one.
  ; Unchanged from the vector this used to be written as.
  Yaw = 219
  StrengthPercent = 100
  ; Just over half. Below about 40 the shadowed side of everything goes to a
  ; silhouette; above about 70 the relief the pitch buys is washed out again.
  AmbientPercent = 55
  ; Warm sun, cold shade. The oldest trick there is for making a flat palette
  ; look like it has air in it, and it costs nothing.
  Colour = 0xFFF7E6
  AmbientTint = 0xE6E6FF
End

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The dark.
;
; Three states, and they are meant to be told apart at a glance: never been
; there (nothing drawn at all -- not the floor, not the walls, not what is
; standing on them), been there and left (dim: the walls are still drawn so the
; way back can be found, and the engine's own fog has already taken the monsters
; out of the snapshot), and in sight now (everything).
;
; LineOfSight is the important one. Without it the light is a circle that does
; not care what it is shining through, so standing in a corridor lights the rooms
; on both sides of it and there is nothing left to discover. With it, a straight
; line is drawn from the hero to every cell and stone stops it -- the wall itself
; is seen, what is behind it is not. Pure arithmetic over the grid the pathfinder
; already keeps: no shader, and nothing the simulation can feel.
;
; Tint is what the dark is made of. Black is a hole in the screen; a cold grey
; blue is a room somebody has stopped lighting, which is the thing being drawn.
;
; How it is drawn: the dark is a TEXTURE, stretched over the whole map as one
; sheet and blended over everything under it -- floor, walls, chests, whatever is
; standing in a room he has left. It used to be a brightness per cell painted flat
; across every tile in that cell, and however carefully the cells themselves were
; softened the floor still came out as a field of ten-unit squares. A sheet has
; its own resolution and the card fills in between its texels, so the edge of the
; light is smooth at the size of a pixel rather than the size of a tile. No shader
; anywhere in it.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Fog
  LineOfSight = Yes
  ; The three shades, as percentages of a room in full sight. Unseen at 0 is the
  ; black a crawler wants; raise it and the whole floor plan is faintly there from
  ; the first frame, which is a different game. Remembered is low enough to read
  ; as memory rather than as light, high enough to find the way back through.
  UnseenPercent = 0
  RememberedPercent = 30
  VisiblePercent = 100
  ; How many cells the edge of the light is smeared over. One is the old hard
  ; staircase softened by a single cell; two is a torch rather than a spotlight.
  SoftenCells = 2
  ; How fast the dark gives way, as a share of what is left each second. Per
  ; second rather than per frame, or a faster machine would open the map faster.
  OpenPerSecond = 7
  ; How many texels across the sheet is, whatever size the map is. Nothing to do
  ; with cells: this is the resolution the dark is DRAWN at, and it is the number
  ; that decides whether the edge of the light is smooth or stepped. 256 over a
  ; dungeon floor is several texels to a cell. Higher costs a little more to push
  ; to the card each frame and nothing else.
  TextureSize = 256
  ; The colour of nothing: cold, and slightly blue, so unlit stone reads as stone
  ; rather than as a gap. Also the window's own background, exactly -- the sheet
  ; at full strength and the void beyond the map are the same dark, and any
  ; difference between them draws the map's outline on the screen.
  Tint = 0x121821
End

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What the floor is built from.
;
; The kit is Kenney's Modular Dungeon Kit (CC0). Its pieces are a floor tile and
; walls that stand on a tile's EDGE — so a wall is drawn on the line between open
; ground and stone, which is exactly where the pathfinder stops the hero. The
; picture and the collision are then the same wall.
;
; TileSize is what the kit was modelled at. A cell is 10 world units, so a kit
; authored at 4 is scaled by 2.5 — the only number here that has to be right.
;
; Naming no Floor turns the whole thing off and the dungeon is drawn in plain
; coloured blocks again, which is what it looked like before any of this.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tiles
  Floor = models/tiles/dungeon/floor.gltf
  Wall = models/tiles/dungeon/wall.gltf
  Stairs = models/tiles/dungeon/stairs.gltf
  TileSize = 4
  ; How tall a wall piece stands, in the same units. It is what the lid over the
  ; rock is laid at: without one you look over the wall into an empty hole, and
  ; the room reads as a house with the roof off.
  WallHeight = 4
  ; This kit's wall is a slab a unit thick, centred on its own origin, so it is
  ; pushed back half a unit to put its face on the boundary. Measured off the
  ; model: x spans -2..2, z spans -0.5..0.5, y starts at 0 — so it needs no lift.
  WallShift = -0.5
  ; No corner post: this kit's corner piece is a length of wall bent round a
  ; right angle rather than the quarter-tile plug the notch wants, and a bent
  ; wall dropped into the notch stands across both walls that meet there.
End

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What all of it is allowed to cost.
;
; Ceilings, not targets. Fifty arrows in the air, each with a hundred sparks and
; a light of its own, is five thousand particles and fifty dynamic lights -- and
; dynamic lights are the expensive kind. Past a ceiling a shot flies plainer: no
; light, or no trail, but the same shot going to the same place.
;
; MaxLights is what the terrain shader reads, which is eight. More than that would
; still light the creatures, drawn as they are with the engine's own lighting, and
; would silently stop lighting the floor -- which is the half worth having, so the
; two numbers are kept equal.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
EffectBudget
  ; ★ EIGHT, and the floor's own shader was widened to take eight to match. A
  ; light the floor could not see would light the skeleton standing on it and
  ; leave the stone under his feet dark, which reads as a light that is broken.
  ; This is the most expensive thing on the page: every light is another pass
  ; over everything it reaches. Past it an effect burns without lighting the room
  ; and keeps everything else.
  MaxLights = 8
  MaxPerEffect = 8
  MaxBursts = 8
  ; How many skill rings may be open across the floor at once. Its own ceiling
  ; rather than a share of the bursts', because it is a different resource: a
  ; burst is particles and a light, a ring is a vertex buffer and two materials.
  ; Six is a whirlwind, a nova and a charge overlapping with room to spare. Past
  ; it the next skill keeps its fire, its light and its damage and loses a
  ; decoration, which is the cheapest thing in the room to lose.
  MaxRings = 6
  ; Nothing beyond this from the camera gets a light or a trail. The hero's own
  ; sight is 70 and the camera sits back from him, so this reaches comfortably
  ; past anything he can see.
  MaxDistance = 320
  ; Every particle burning at once, across every layer of every effect. Each is
  ; worked out on the graphics card from where and when it was born, so what
  ; this really limits is how much of the screen is being painted over more than
  ; once -- the part of a fight that makes a laptop warm. A meteor landing is
  ; about two hundred; this is four meteors, a nova and a fireball at once.
  MaxParticles = 1500
End

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; How a fight FEELS, as against what it costs.
;
; ShakeScale is every knock of the camera at once, against what each effect asked
; for: 1 as written, 0 for a camera that never moves. One number rather than a
; ShakePower zeroed on every effect, because whether the screen moves is a
; question about the player rather than about any one skill.
;
; And a creature that is hit goes white for an instant. The number coming off it
; says how much; the flash says WHICH, in a crowd of six round a nova. Strength is
; how far towards the colour, 0 for no flash at all; Seconds is the whole of it,
; since the flash is there at once and fades as a square. Only the other side's
; creatures flash -- the hero's own hurt is his bar and his portrait.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
HitFeel
  ShakeScale = 1
  HitFlashColour = 0xFFFFFF
  HitFlashSeconds = 0.14
  HitFlashStrength = 0.85
  ; And a shot bursts only if it struck somebody: a blow has to land on the other
  ; side, that same frame, this near to where the shot was last drawn, and the
  ; burst goes on the body it struck, which is where its blast is centred. A shot
  ; that meets a wall or runs out of flight hurts nobody, so it bursts nowhere. A
  ; fireball covers four units a frame, so a few frames of it and a body's width.
  ; A mark that lies where it was put -- the meteor's -- lands whatever is under it.
  StrikeWithin = 30
End

; ===========================================================================
; What the dungeon sounds like.
;
; The client raises moments by name -- an arrow left a bow, a floor changed, a
; key was pressed -- and has never heard of a bow. What each moment sounds like
; is here, and adding one is a block rather than a line of Java. That division
; is the same one the models follow, and for the same reason: client3d draws
; four games and cannot hold any of their opinions.
;
; A name may be dotted. The client asks for `died.Boss` and takes `died` when
; nobody wrote the first -- so a game is as particular as it wants to be and no
; more, and no creature's name appears in the client.
;
; Channel is which knob turns it down: Effects, Voice, Ui or Music. Four rather
; than one because the same person wants them at different volumes -- keeping
; the music low while still hearing what is behind you is an ordinary thing to
; want, and so is silencing a hero who will not stop talking.
;
; Positional says whether it happened somewhere. A shot did; a menu click did
; not, and giving it a position would make it quieter when the camera moved.
;
; Several File lines means several ways it can sound, and the client never plays
; the same one twice running. That matters most where it is heard most: the bow
; goes hundreds of times in a run, and one unvarying sample stops being a bow
; and becomes a click.
;
; Gain is per cue because four packs recorded by four people are not mastered to
; each other. GapSeconds is the least time between two of the same moment -- a
; footstep needs a stride, or a walk is a buzz.
; ===========================================================================
Audio
  ; One voice at a time. A player clicking around the floor gives an order every
  ; frame he likes; she answers the first and then keeps quiet.
  VoiceGapSeconds = 1.6
End
